> I don't know for whom you are talking if you say "we all know".

Anyone with half a brain in their head?

> Objective data seems to suggest the opposite. They *are* the easiest.

Except that the data you provide isn't objective, and as such must be
tempered with common sense -- something you seem to be sorely lacking.

It's not objective because the classes are not randomly selected, they
are self-selected by the players.  The prevailing advice given to
newbies is "Play a Valkyrie first", and this is correspondingly
reflected in the significant stat jump for Valkyries.  It's also
reflected in the fact that Valkyries (arguably _the_ easiest class in
the game) do not have a particularly high ascension ratio -- because
newbies Do Not Ascend.  They die.

Given that the data is obtained by self-selection, this can only be a
single point of information and never a conclusive demonstration.  If a
server were set up that only allowed players to play random classes,
THEN by filtering out the #quits we would be able to obtain a much more
objective set of data... though we might just find that players who got
a class they didn't like just #quit out of it, ruining our dataset.  But
as it stands now, Cavemen and Knights are also the least-played classes.
 If you look at statnethack.txt, you will also see that the average
score for a Caveman is more than 400,000 points higher than any other
class -- providing more support for the statement that only experienced
players play Cavemen in the first place.

There's even an argument to be made that with such a small sample size
for those classes (half of the third-fewest games played), you can't
draw _any_ conclusions about a Caveman or Knight.

> That also matches my experience, it's not an academical statement.

You're an experienced player who picks games completely at random.  Your
bell curve isn't going to look the same as a newbie's, and there are far
more newbies than there are of you.

>> Yes, a bad player will die with any class.  Some classes are easier to
